        <h1 class="hero__h1">Do You Need a Lawyer for Divorce in the UAE?</h1>
        <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated September 2026</p>
        <p class="hero__sub">
          Not always. UAE law lets you file and appear in a family case yourself, and plenty of agreed
          divorces finish without a lawyer. Below: when doing it yourself is safe, when it is a false
          saving, the middle options, and what each route costs. Agreed on everything? Read our
          <a href="/en/mutual-consent-divorce-uae/">mutual consent divorce guide</a> too.
        </p>
        <ul class="hero__trust">
          <li>✓ Plain answer, no sales pitch</li>
          <li>✓ Dubai, Abu Dhabi &amp; northern emirates</li>
          <li>✓ Decision table by situation</li>
          <li>✓ DIY vs lawyer costs in AED</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__form">
        <LeadForm lang="en" variant="hero" heading="Not Sure? Talk It Through Free" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Short answer -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
      <h2 id="answer-heading">Do You Need a Lawyer for Divorce in the UAE? The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>
        No law requires you to hire a lawyer to get divorced in the UAE. At first instance you can file
        and appear in person in every emirate: Article 52 of the Civil Procedure Law (Federal Decree-Law
        No. 42 of 2022) lets the parties attend themselves or through an attorney, who can be a lawyer or
        a relative up to the fourth degree. Whether you should go without one depends on three things.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Your track.</strong> Muslim couples go through Family Guidance and Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024; non-Muslims use the civil, no-fault track under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 or the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court.</li>
        <li><strong>Whether you agree.</strong> An agreed divorce is mostly paperwork; a contested one means hearings, evidence and pleadings in Arabic.</li>
        <li><strong>What is at stake.</strong> Children, a home, a business or a spouse abroad raise the cost of a mistake far above the cost of advice.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">Yes</span>
          <span class="stat-label">you may file and appear yourself in a family case</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">4th</span>
          <span class="stat-label">degree relative who can appear for you instead</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">30 days</span>
          <span class="stat-label">to appeal a family judgment</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">AED 5,000</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Abu Dhabi civil divorce application fee</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Dubai</h3>
          <p>
            Dubai Courts hear both tracks: Muslim couples start at Family Guidance, non-Muslims file with
            the Personal Status Court. Arabic is the court language (Article 5, Civil Procedure Law), so
            foreign documents need legal translation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Abu Dhabi</h3>
          <p>
            Muslims: Family Guidance, then the ADJD Personal Status Court. Non-Muslims: the Civil Family
            Court, where you may appear in person or appoint a lawyer (Article 45 of its 2022 regulations)
            and English documents are accepted.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Northern emirates</h3>
          <p>
            Sharjah, Ajman, Umm Al Quwain and Fujairah use the federal courts; Ras Al Khaimah has its own.
            The same federal laws apply, so you can appear in person there too.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>How this site earns money</h3>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is a referral service, not a law firm, and earns when readers ask to be put in touch
          with a lawyer. That is exactly why this page says plainly where you can manage without one. The
          free consultation offered here is a chance to talk your situation through, nothing more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who can represent you -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="audience-heading">
      <h2 id="audience-heading">Who Can Speak for You in a UAE Family Court?</h2>
      <p>
        Three kinds of people can appear in a UAE family court: you, a close relative you authorise, and a
        licensed advocate. Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 on the legal profession, in force since
        2 January 2023, limits representing others in court to lawyers on the Roll of Practising Lawyers
        (Article 6), but lets you authorise a spouse, in-law or blood relative up to the fourth degree
        instead (Article 8). As your spouse is the other side, that means a parent, sibling, adult child,
        aunt, uncle or cousin.
      </p>
      <p>
        The federal roll is for UAE nationals (Article 13). Article 10 lets a non-Emirati lawyer be
        licensed only after at least 15 years in practice, as a partner in a licensed foreign firm, and
        only before specialised circuits, with Muslim family and personal status cases carved out. In
        Dubai, the Legal Affairs Department licenses advocates and legal consultants. Legal consultants,
        which is how most foreign-qualified lawyers here work, can advise and draft but cannot plead or
        represent anyone in court (Article 59).
      </p>
      <p>
        The exception is the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court. Under ADJD Decision No. 21 of 2024, foreign
        lawyers who meet strict conditions, including 15 years in practice and working through an Abu
        Dhabi licensed firm, can join a Roll of Foreign Lawyers and appear there, and only there. The DIFC
        and ADGM Courts do not grant divorces at all; see
        <a href="/en/difc-divorce-dubai/">why the DIFC cannot divorce you</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Advocate</h3>
          <p>
            On a roll of practising lawyers, with rights of audience. Can file, plead and appear for you
            under a power of attorney. Ask for the licence number and the issuing authority.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Legal consultant</h3>
          <p>
            Licensed to advise and draft, not to plead. Well suited to reviewing an agreement or giving an
            opinion. If your case needs hearings, confirm that an advocate will appear.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Decision table -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="decision-heading">
      <h2 id="decision-heading">Lawyer or No Lawyer? A Decision Table by Situation</h2>
      <p>
        Most couples who agree on everything and have no children or significant assets can manage
        without a lawyer; once children, money, a dispute or another country is involved, the balance
        tips towards getting help.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Mutual consent, no children, no significant shared assets</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Usually fine without</span></td>
              <td>What Family Guidance and the civil route are built for. A one-off review is cheap insurance.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Non-Muslim no-fault divorce in Abu Dhabi, nothing to divide</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Usually fine without</span></td>
              <td>Bilingual forms, English accepted, filed through a typing centre.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Enforcing a clear existing order, such as unpaid maintenance</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ok">Usually fine without</span></td>
              <td>Get help if the payer hides income or has left the UAE. See <a href="/en/unpaid-alimony-enforcement-uae/">enforcing unpaid alimony</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mutual consent with children</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Lawyer recommended</span></td>
              <td>Custody schedule, travel, school fees and housing need precise drafting.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Khula (wife asks, husband accepts compensation)</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Lawyer recommended</span></td>
              <td>Usually means giving up the mahr and iddah maintenance. See <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula in the UAE</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>One spouse lives abroad</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Lawyer recommended</span></td>
              <td>Service abroad, powers of attorney and recognition in the other country.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Your spouse has hired a lawyer</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--rec">Lawyer recommended</span></td>
              <td>Their lawyer drafts for them. Get at least your own review.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Contested divorce: one spouse refuses, or terms are disputed</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>Hearings, evidence, expert reports and pleadings in Arabic.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Divorce for harm (darar)</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>Harm must be proven or the case is dismissed; continuing discord then goes to arbitrators, who have up to 60 days (Articles 71 and 72 of the 2024 law). See <a href="/en/divorce-for-harm-uae/">divorce for harm</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Domestic violence</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>Call 999 in an emergency. Protection orders exist under Federal Decree-Law No. 13 of 2024. See <a href="/en/domestic-violence-divorce-uae/">domestic violence and divorce</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Property, a business or significant savings</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>Accounting experts, valuations and disclosure decide the numbers. See <a href="/en/property-division-divorce-uae/">property division</a>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>You want your home-country law applied (non-Muslims)</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>The foreign law must be put before the court in translated, attested form.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Appeal</td>
              <td><span class="verdict verdict--ess">Lawyer essential</span></td>
              <td>30-day deadlines, and a cassation appeal must be signed by a lawyer admitted to that court (Articles 161, 178 and 179 of the Civil Procedure Law).</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          A guide, not legal advice. &ldquo;Recommended&rdquo; can mean one review rather than full
          representation; see the middle options below.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Unsure which row fits? The deciding question is usually whether your case is genuinely
        uncontested; see <a href="/en/contested-vs-uncontested-divorce-uae/">contested vs uncontested divorce</a>,
        and the <a href="/en/divorce-for-expats-in-uae/">expat divorce guide</a> if a spouse is abroad.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Uncontested in Dubai step by step -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="dubai-heading">
      <h2 id="dubai-heading">Uncontested Divorce in Dubai Without a Lawyer: Step by Step</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, you can complete an uncontested divorce in Dubai without a lawyer; the steps depend on your
        track. Gather your papers first with our
        <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">divorce documents checklist</a>, because an unattested
        marriage certificate is the most common reason a DIY filing stalls.
      </p>

      <h3>Muslim couples: Family Guidance and an approved agreement</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Open a Family Guidance file</h3>
          <p>Family Guidance is the mandatory first step in most Muslim personal status cases and usually takes a few weeks. Either spouse registers the case at Dubai Courts with the marriage certificate, passports and Emirates IDs.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Attend together</h3>
          <p>The counsellor tries reconciliation first. If you both still want to divorce, set out the terms you have agreed. See the <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance stage explained</a>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Sign the written agreement</h3>
          <p>The terms go into a report signed by both spouses and the counsellor. It is in Arabic, so read it with a translation you trust before signing.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The judge approves it</h3>
          <p>Once approved by the supervising judge, it has the force of an executory instrument (Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024): it can be enforced like a judgment.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Collect the paperwork</h3>
          <p>Attest the divorce papers if you need them abroad; see <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation</a>.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Non-Muslim couples: the civil route</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>File the standard form</h3>
          <p>Either or both spouses file with the Personal Status Court under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022. There is no Family Guidance stage (Article 3).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Attach translated documents</h3>
          <p>Attested marriage certificate, passports, Emirates IDs and any written agreement, with Arabic translations by a legal translator.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Divorce at the first hearing</h3>
          <p>No reason or blame is needed (Article 7). The other spouse is served and the court rules at the first hearing (Articles 3 and 8).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Settle money and children</h3>
          <p>Put any written agreement before the court so it forms part of the case. Otherwise a divorced wife can claim alimony after the judgment (Article 9), and joint custody is the default (Article 10).</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>Filing in Abu Dhabi instead?</h3>
        <p>
          At the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, the bilingual form goes in through an Abu Dhabi typing
          centre, the fee is AED 5,000, the hearing is set at least 30 days after approval, and the judge
          grants the divorce at that first hearing (Articles 12 to 14 of the ADJD 2022 regulations). See
          <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>What you still pay without a lawyer</h3>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Item</th><th>Indicative cost</th><th>Source or note</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Court fees, Dubai</td><td>AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000</td><td>Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 on judicial fees, as amended</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Civil divorce, Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</td><td>AED 5,000</td><td>Article 13, ADJD 2022 regulations; no translation of English documents</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Legal translation into Arabic</td><td>Roughly AED 50 to 250 per page</td><td>Translation-office price guides; rare languages cost more</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Attesting a foreign marriage certificate</td><td>AED 150 per document at MOFA</td><td>Plus legalisation costs in the issuing country</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typing office</td><td>Varies</td><td>Ask for the price before they start</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Indicative; confirm current fees when you file. Full breakdown:
          <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">total cost of divorce</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Where doing it yourself usually goes wrong</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Filing in the wrong court, for example expecting the DIFC Courts to grant a divorce.</li>
        <li>A foreign marriage certificate that was never legalised and attested, or no Arabic translation.</li>
        <li>Agreeing the divorce but not the details: holidays, travel, school fees and rent.</li>
        <li>An agreement the court never approved, or a missed 30-day appeal deadline.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The full Dubai process, including timelines, is in
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file for divorce in Dubai</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What can go wrong -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="risks-heading">
      <h2 id="risks-heading">What Can Go Wrong Without a Lawyer</h2>
      <p>
        The real risk in a do-it-yourself divorce is rarely the filing; it is signing an agreement that is
        vague, one-sided or unenforceable. Article 8 of the 2024 law says an approved Family Guidance
        agreement cannot be challenged unless it breaches the law, so &ldquo;we will sort the details out
        later&rdquo; rarely works.
      </p>

      <h3>A badly drafted settlement agreement</h3>
      <p>
        The usual gaps: custody with no weekly schedule, holidays or handovers; no travel clause, although
        Article 116 of the 2024 law requires the other parent&rsquo;s written consent to travel with the
        child, failing which a court can permit up to 60 days a year; child support with no amount, due
        date or school fees; and nothing on housing, although Article 119 makes whoever pays child
        maintenance cover the custodian&rsquo;s rent. Our
        <a href="/en/divorce-settlement-uae/">settlement agreement checklist</a> lists what a workable
        agreement needs.
      </p>

      <h3>Claims you did not know you could make</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>Muslim wives (2024 law)</h3>
          <p>
            Iddah maintenance and housing after a revocable divorce (Article 101); a consolation payment of
            up to one year&rsquo;s maintenance if the husband divorces without any request or fault on her
            part (Article 102); and any deferred mahr, due on an irrevocable divorce (Article 46). Khula
            usually gives some of this up. See <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr on divorce</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>Non-Muslims (civil track)</h3>
          <p>
            Article 9 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 weighs years of marriage, the wife&rsquo;s age,
            both spouses&rsquo; finances as assessed by an accounting expert, fault and harm. In Abu Dhabi,
            the 2022 regulations add a list of factors (Article 16) and a guide based on a percentage of
            the husband&rsquo;s last monthly income times the years of marriage (Article 18), which the
            court can raise or lower.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        None of this is automatic in an agreement: if the draft is silent, you have usually given it up.
        Background in our guides to <a href="/en/alimony-uae/">alimony in the UAE</a> and the
        <a href="/en/iddah-waiting-period-uae/">iddah waiting period</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Enforceability and finality</h3>
      <p>
        Only a court-approved agreement goes straight to enforcement if payments stop; an email or private
        contract does not. Finality cuts both ways: under Article 49 of the ADJD 2022 regulations, a Civil
        Family Court judgment on post-divorce financial rights below AED 500,000 is final once issued, open
        to challenge only on narrow grounds such as jurisdiction.
      </p>

      <h3>Recognition abroad</h3>
      <p>
        A UAE divorce is not automatically registered at home. Most countries want an attested judgment,
        and some, the UK among them, can refuse recognition if the other spouse had no proper notice or
        chance to take part. See <a href="/en/uae-divorce-recognised-uk/">UK recognition of UAE divorces</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Middle options -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="middle-heading">
      <h2 id="middle-heading">Middle Options Between Doing It Yourself and Full Representation</h2>
      <p>
        You do not have to choose between doing everything yourself and a full retainer: Article 49 of
        Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 expressly allows a lawyer to be hired just to review an
        agreement, give an opinion, handle one stage or take one procedural step.
      </p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>One consultation</h3>
          <p>
            Often free; a paid strategy session is indicatively AED 500 to 1,500. Ask one question: can I
            safely do this myself? See <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-consultation-fee-uae/">consultation fees</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Agreement review only</h3>
          <p>
            You negotiate; a lawyer marks up the draft before anyone signs. The cheapest insurance when
            children or money are involved.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Limited-scope help</h3>
          <p>
            One piece of work: drafting, one hearing, an appeal or an enforcement step. Get the scope and
            fee in writing first, which Article 46 requires anyway.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Family Guidance and mediation</h3>
          <p>
            Counsellors record an agreement but are neutral, so they will not say whether it is fair to
            you. In Abu Dhabi&rsquo;s civil court, spouses can also agree on a registered family arbitrator.
            See <a href="/en/mediated-divorce-uae/">mediated divorce</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Legal aid</h3>
          <p>
            ADJD legal aid can include representation for applicants who qualify on income and merits, and
            Dubai Courts&rsquo; Shoor programme has offered free consultations. See
            <a href="/en/divorce-legal-aid-uae/">divorce legal aid</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Typing centres</h3>
          <p>
            They fill in and file forms but cannot advise you: legal advice is reserved for licensed
            practitioners under Article 6 of the 2022 law.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Cost comparison -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cost-heading">
      <h2 id="cost-heading">What It Costs: DIY vs Limited Help vs Full Representation</h2>
      <p>
        Indicatively, a simple do-it-yourself divorce in Dubai costs around AED 1,000 to 3,000, limited
        help adds roughly AED 500 to 5,000, and full representation starts at about AED 8,000 for an
        uncontested case. The DIY range is built from the fees above; the lawyer ranges come from law-firm
        cost guides.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Route</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Indicative cost</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>DIY, Dubai</td><td>Court fees, translation, attestation, typing</td><td>AED 1,000 to 3,000 in total</td></tr>
            <tr><td>DIY, Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</td><td>Application fee, few extras</td><td>From about AED 5,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>One consultation</td><td>Scoping call or strategy session</td><td>Free, or AED 500 to 1,500</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Agreement review only</td><td>A lawyer reads and marks up your draft</td><td>Roughly AED 1,500 to 5,000 (two to four hours at junior to mid-level rates)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Full representation, uncontested</td><td>Drafting, filing and hearings, usually a fixed fee</td><td>AED 8,000 to 25,000 (simple Abu Dhabi civil cases from about AED 5,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Full representation, contested</td><td>Hearings, experts, custody or money disputes</td><td>AED 15,000 to 80,000; complex cases AED 75,000 to 150,000 or more</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Indicative only; lawyer fees sit on top of court costs. Details in our
          <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Weigh the fee against what is at stake: a review costing a few thousand dirhams is small next to
        years of child support or a share of a home, and when nothing like that is on the table, spending
        nothing on a lawyer is a reasonable choice. Every cost from filing to attestation is in
        <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">how much a divorce costs in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What a lawyer does -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="role-heading">
      <h2 id="role-heading">If You Hire a Lawyer: What They Actually Do in a UAE Divorce</h2>
      <p>
        A UAE divorce lawyer&rsquo;s job is to put your case in the right court under the right law, value
        your claims before you negotiate, write what the court needs in Arabic, speak for you at hearings
        and make any deal enforceable. In practice that means:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Choosing the court and law: Sharia or civil track, Dubai or Abu Dhabi, home-country law or not.</li>
        <li>Valuing mahr, iddah maintenance and the consolation payment, or civil alimony, housing and child support.</li>
        <li>Drafting the agreement and written submissions in Arabic.</li>
        <li>Appearing for you under a power of attorney, so you may not need to attend every session.</li>
        <li>Seeking urgent orders: temporary maintenance (Article 100 of the 2024 law), travel bans or protection orders.</li>
        <li>Handling the court&rsquo;s accounting expert, enforcement and any appeal within 30 days.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Choosing one is covered in
        <a href="/en/how-to-choose-a-divorce-lawyer-uae/">how to choose a divorce lawyer in the UAE</a>:
        check the licence and who will appear in court, ask for a fixed fee if your case is agreed, and get
        the retainer agreement in writing before work starts. For Dubai options, see
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyers in Dubai</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-inline">
        <p>Want a second opinion on whether you need a lawyer at all?</p>
        <a href="#contact" class="btn btn--primary">Ask for a Free Consultation</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Related -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
